    "body": "I’ve reached out directly to three peers (some on the thread) but, unfortunately, couldn’t get to more clarity off-thread. (Still, many thanks for the feedback on and off the thread!)\r\n\r\nHere’s the best I can currently summarize the issue:\r\n\r\nThe way the spec and previous responses put it, there are actually two readings of “[…] a start tag must never be omitted if it has any attributes”:\r\n\r\n1. It’s *possible* to remove a start tag, but retain its attributes.\r\n2. It’s *not possible* to remove a start tag without also losing its attributes.\r\n\r\nWhat’s more, this could come with different motivations:\r\n\r\n* Related to 1., the motivation for the spec to call this out to avoid parsing issues. (This seems dramatic, and worth expanding on.)\r\n* Related to 2., the motivation for the spec to make clear that more would change than just removing a containing element, and that it also affects any attributes defined. (As this is observable when removing just a start tag, but not its attributes [`< attribute=value>`], in an HTML document, this seems trivial, and hence suggested it may be worth dropping.)\r\n\r\nI understand the spec so that the case is 2.—but the way the sentence is phrased, it seems it’s saying 1. (which had me wonder and file this issue).\r\n\r\nTo avoid ambiguity, the wording benefits from being tweaked—it’s probably a small edit, that with some reordering (bring the statement closer to the other “may or may not be omitted” statements) could make the section clearer and easier to read.\r\n\r\nIf this is hard to explain and resolve because it’s clear to “everyone else,” I hope this shows how it can be understood differently.",
